# Future SCION Internet

The predominant workload of web browsing and content delivery is poised to persist and even intensify in the next generation internet.
So do the core technologies that enable it, HTTP and DNS, which will continue to - stand at the heart of it/ assume a pivotal role/ serve as a cornerstone/ take on a central function.

Apart from/ Beside the continutiy of these core technologies in principal, the transition to SCION provides the opportunity,
 to retire/clean-sweap/clear up/  outdated/anachronistic weak points of today's Internet like i.e. unencrypted DNS (Do53) that have become an increasing burden.

SCION also takes a turn at the Internet's web PKI and ...  
.. where today's Internet falls short
- improved transparency . . who issued and signed a TLS certificate for a given domain
- multiple trust anchors

Support for unencrypted Do53 will be phased out in favor of DNS over Encryption (DoE), ensuring end-to-end privacy for all DNS queries.  


The core technologies in today's Internet that enable the predominant workload of web browsing HTTP and DNS are poised to persist and even intensify in the future Internet. (und weiterhin eine wichtige schluesselrolle uebernehmen werden ..)

This example shows how the predominant technologies in today's internet, HTTPS and DNS, enable web browsing also in the next-generation SCION Internet.

Although the essential/fundamental/principal continuity of core technologies (HTTPs and DNS) 

.. this provides the opportunity to retire some outdated burdens like i.e. unencrypted DNS (Do53).


This example showcases how web technologies will evolve with the adoption of SCION in the future internet.

## Details

In order to resolve domain names to SCION addresses, the hosts run a modified SCION capable sdns recursive resolver (listening at `127.0.0.127`) instead of the default one provided by docker (listening at `127.0.0.11`).
Because most browsers are currently neither SCION nor HTTP/3 capable, a forward proxy is required to access native websites, hosted in the SCION Internet (web server listening on a SCION address).
It translates HTTP/2 requests from the browser (client) received over legacy TCP/IP transport and forwards them to the web servers over SCION QUIC and vice versa with the response.

- port forwarding into/out of the emulation

## DNS Support for SCION

To achieve backward compatibility and low overheads, the
TXT format was simply chosen as the Resource Record (RR)
format for SCION addresses (instead of A/AAAA for IP).
The  textual representation is of the form `Isolation Domain (ISD)-Autonomous System (AS), IP address`.
An example is:
```
perrig.inf.ethz.ch. 273 IN TXT "scion=17-ffaa:0:1102,129.132.121.164"
```
the SCION record of the Internet site of the NetSec working
group at ETH Zurich. This allows a domain to retain its
existing IPv4/IPv6 reachability in the conventional Internet
and at the same time already migrate independently into the
new Internet architecture. This is important in order to be
able to bootstrap SCION on a large scale.
End hosts could prefer SCION services/Internet sites over conventional ones,
if already offered, to benefit from their advantages.



### Library Support
As of now, the most convenient way to resolve SCION
addresses for domain names is to use the function
`pan.ResolveUDPAddress()`.

This function first checks `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/scion/hosts` (steps 1 & 2) and
then falls back to the `net.Resolver` to search for a `TXT RR`
with a SCION address for the domain name (step 3).
However, this is done without any privacy via UDP.

Depending on the option chosen in VI-E, additional
steps can be inserted between steps 2 and 3.
These steps can either query the locally running SDNS resolver at
`127.0.0.1:5553` via UDP or
(if it’s not running/accessible there) as a fallback,
query the central remote resolver via SCION DoQ under its SCION address,
 which could be configured by default, for instance, in `/etc/scion/hosts`.
This approach would at least attempt to ensure DNS privacy before
resorting to the `net.Resolver`.

This has been implemented for Option 1 from VI-E.

### Resolving SCION Addresses

retrieve the SCION address (TXT RR) for the domain name `dummy.luki.test.home.`.
```
sdig @19-ffaa:1:1094,[127.0.0.1] -p 8853 IN TXT dummy.luki.test.home.
```

## Resolving IP addresses over SCION
retrieve the IP address (A RR) of the domain name `dummy.luki.test.home.` from
a NS that listens on a SCION address `@19-ffaa:1:1094,[127.0.0.1]`.
```
sdig @19-ffaa:1:1094,[127.0.0.1] -p 8853 IN A dummy.luki.test.home.
```